  "proposedReplacementNote": "The contradictory row gives the denial of the original; the other populated cells are presented as consequences of the original. In modern set semantics allowing empty terms, the simple converse of A(B,C) and both contrapositives of E(B,C) require B to be nonempty. The forms complementing both terms or the subject alone require different existence premises: from A(B,C), non-C must be nonempty; from E(B,C), C must be nonempty. These last premises do not follow merely from B being nonempty. The other populated consequences need no additional existence premise. The proofs on displayed pp190–191 give no separate statement that the required C or non-C class exists."
